## 2. REPAIR THE COMPLETE EXAMINATION LIFECYCLE

### 2.1 Tutors must see and manage examinations they own

At present, tutors need a reliable way to find examinations they previously created, whether those examinations are drafts, scheduled, published, closed or completed.

Implement a clear examination-management workspace with appropriate status filters.

Tutors must be able to:

- View examinations they created.
- Open an existing examination and inspect its questions, marks, assignment targets, class, schedule and publication status.
- Edit an existing examination.
- Save changes to the existing examination.
- Change an eligible examination's status.
- Publish an examination.
- Republish an eligible examination after making changes.
- Preview the examination before publishing.
- Delete examinations they own when permitted by the examination's current state.
- View assigned schools and classes that they are authorised to manage.
- See the examinations they are responsible for and their relevant access codes.

The existing-examination list must load real persisted records, not mock data or a local-only list.

### 2.2 Editing must never accidentally create duplicate examinations

This requirement is critical.

When a tutor opens an existing examination, modifies it and clicks Save, the system must update the existing examination record rather than creating a second record.

Investigate the current create/update logic carefully. Check whether the form resets its examination ID, whether the save handler always invokes the create endpoint, or whether publishing creates a new document.

Correct the underlying cause.

Expected behaviour:

1. Creating a genuinely new examination creates one examination record.
2. Opening an existing examination retains its document ID.
3. Saving changes updates the same examination record.
4. Republishing updates the existing record and its eligible status fields.
5. Repeatedly clicking Save or Publish does not create additional examination documents.
6. Refreshing the browser does not make the examination disappear or duplicate.
7. The same examination remains associated with its intended school, class, questions and schedule.

Use the application's existing schema and server-side authorisation approach. Ensure the backend distinguishes authorised updates to existing records from the creation of new ones.

### 2.3 Editing, publishing and republishing must be state-aware

Create a proper, consistent lifecycle covering the statuses already supported by the application, such as draft, scheduled, published/active, closed and completed.

Do not introduce conflicting status names or allow the interface and backend to disagree.

A tutor should be able to edit and republish an examination where the lifecycle allows it.

However, protect historical attempts. If a published examination already has student attempts, changing its questions or point allocations must not silently corrupt previously submitted work, alter a historical report unexpectedly or change which answer key was used for previous grading.

Inspect how the existing application stores attempts and their relationship to an examination definition. Implement the smallest safe solution, using the existing versioning or snapshot mechanism if one exists. Where necessary, preserve an immutable definition or revision for submitted attempts.

Changes to future attempts must not rewrite historical results without an explicit, authorised regrading workflow.

Do not allow a completed examination to be reopened or reset through an accidental status update if the existing lifecycle is designed to prevent that. Respect the intended state transitions.

### 2.4 Examination deletion must preserve integrity

Tutors may delete examinations they created, subject to ownership and lifecycle restrictions.

If an examination has no attempts and the current design permits deletion, allow deletion through a properly authorised operation.

If student attempts, released results, reports or audit records already exist, prefer the application's supported archive/soft-delete mechanism where available. Otherwise, implement a safe deletion policy that prevents historical records from becoming orphaned or inaccessible.

A delete action must not permit one tutor to delete another tutor's examination.

The interface must show appropriate confirmation before destructive actions.

## 3. FIX SCHOOL ASSIGNMENT AND TUTOR SCHOOL SCOPE

### 3.1 Tutors must only see their assigned schools

A tutor must see only the schools that are actually assigned to that tutor or that the tutor is otherwise explicitly authorised to manage through the established platform relationship.

Apply this consistently to:

- School selectors when creating or editing examinations.
- Examination lists and filters.
- Class selectors.
- Examination access codes.
- Student and school examination reports.
- Any school dropdown, search interface or management list within the tutor portal.

Do not simply filter schools in React while leaving unrestricted school retrieval available in backend functions.

The server must derive the tutor's verified identity and permitted school relationships from trusted records. It must not trust a browser-supplied school ID as proof that the tutor owns or manages that school.

Retain legitimate private-tutoring and individual-student workflows.

### 3.2 School administrators must never see other schools' private records

Investigate the existing School portal, especially the school list and examination sections.

A school administrator must not be able to list, browse, select, query or modify another school's private records merely because the records exist in a shared collection.

The school administrator should see the school associated with the authenticated account, not a global directory of partner schools that exposes other schools' identities and data.

Apply this to:

- School lists.
- School profile and settings.
- Examination lists and access codes.
- Student rosters.
- Classes and enrolments.
- Reports and progress records.
- Search and filter endpoints.
- Any shared administrative list displayed within the School portal.

Use the authenticated account's trusted canonical school relationship and the application's existing server-side role checks.

A browser-supplied `schoolId`, URL parameter, query parameter or modified request must not allow a user to switch into another school's scope.

Preserve the platform administrator's legitimate cross-school access and the tutor's legitimate assigned-school access. Do not break existing role boundaries while tightening the School portal.

Add or extend negative security tests proving that one school administrator cannot retrieve another school's records.

## 4. SCHOOL ADMINISTRATORS MUST NOT AUTHOR EXAMINATIONS

The School portal currently exposes examination-related functionality that appears to let a school administrator create or set examinations.

Correct this across both the UI and backend.

School administrators should be able to:

- View their own school's examination history.
- View their own school's upcoming examinations.
- View eligible examination access codes needed to administer an assigned exam.
- See appropriate examination schedules, class assignments and result/report information within their authorised scope.

They must not be able to:

- Create examinations.
- Edit examination questions or answer keys.
- Publish or republish tutor examinations.
- Delete examinations.
- Change examination ownership or assignment targets.
- Access examination-authoring or question-bank management operations reserved for tutors/staff or platform administrators.

Remove or disable inappropriate authoring controls in the School portal navigation and pages. More importantly, enforce the same restrictions inside the relevant Netlify Functions or other server-side endpoints and ensure Firestore rules do not allow an unauthorised bypass.

Do not broaden permissions simply to make a screen work.

## 5. FIX PARTNER SCHOOL NAMES IN EXAMINATION REPORTS

There is a serious reporting error.

When a tutor assigns an examination to a partner school and a student from that school takes it, the examination report sometimes displays **Jaystarbliss** as the partner school.

That is incorrect.

Jaystarbliss is the platform/brand. The report must identify the actual partner school to which the examination was assigned.

Trace the complete data path:

1. Tutor selects a school.
2. The school and class are saved on the examination assignment.
3. Student becomes eligible for the examination.
4. Student starts and completes an attempt.
5. Result is graded and released.
6. School and tutor portals retrieve the result.
7. The student report sheet is generated or downloaded.

Identify why the reporting code is using a generic platform name, an incorrect fallback, or the wrong school field.

Resolve the actual partner-school record using the correct canonical `schoolId` or other established trusted relationship, then obtain the authoritative school name from its record.

Ensure the correct school name appears consistently in:

- Student examination report sheets.
- Tutor examination reports.
- School administrator reports.
- Individual student PDF downloads.
- Bulk-generated report PDFs.
- Excel exports where school identity is included.
- Any downloadable or printable examination result sheet.

Do not hardcode a school name to solve this issue. Do not rely solely on a free-text school name supplied by the browser if a trusted school record is available.

Existing historical examination records may contain legacy school identifiers. Follow the application's existing school-ID compatibility strategy and resolve those records correctly without granting cross-school access.

If a historical record genuinely cannot be resolved, handle that case explicitly rather than silently labelling another school as Jaystarbliss.

## 6. FIX EXAMINATION MARKS, DENOMINATORS AND GRADING

This is another critical problem affecting the integrity of examination reports.

For example, a tutor sets an examination with a total of three available marks. A student earns one mark.

The student report correctly shows:

- Score: 1 out of 3.
- Percentage: approximately 33.33%.

However, the tutor and School portals sometimes show 1 out of 100.

This is incorrect. The denominator must not default to 100 when the actual examination is worth three marks.

### 6.1 Let tutors set marks for each question

Review the existing question editor and grading interfaces.

Tutors should be able to configure the marks or points allocated to each question, within any reasonable restrictions already imposed by the platform.

The total possible mark for an examination must be calculated from its actual question definitions or from the application's established authoritative total-mark field.

Support the application's existing objective-question and manual-grading workflows. Ensure partial marks, question-level marks and accepted answers behave consistently where those features are supported.

Do not introduce a separate grading system that contradicts the existing question model.

### 6.2 Store and calculate the score correctly

Establish one consistent scoring model.

Where appropriate, preserve distinct values for:

- Marks earned.
- Total possible marks.
- Percentage.
- Letter grade or grade band, if configured.
- Examination result/release status.

The percentage must be calculated from the actual total possible marks, not from a hardcoded denominator.

For example:

| Actual result | Required display |
|---|---|
| 1 out of 3 | 1/3; 33.33% |
| 4 out of 10 | 4/10; 40% |
| 18 out of 20 | 18/20; 90% |
| 75 out of 100 | 75/100; 75% |

Use sensible rounding for display only. Do not use rounded percentages as the authoritative values for grading.

If an examination is explicitly configured to use a 100-mark scale, respect that configuration. Do not silently convert every examination to 100 marks.

### 6.3 Keep grades consistent across every portal

The student, tutor and school views must use the same authoritative attempt/result data and calculation logic.

A score must not be interpreted one way in the student portal and another way in the School portal.

Review:

- Automatic grading.
- Manual grading and mark adjustments.
- Timeout and automatic finalisation.
- Answer-key correction and any authorised regrading.
- Result release.
- Examination history.
- Parent results.
- PDF and Excel reports.
- Bulk report exports.

For historical attempts whose `maxScore` or equivalent field is missing, inspect the stored question definition or attempt snapshot and derive the correct denominator from trusted data where possible.

Do not automatically replace a missing denominator with 100. If the historical denominator cannot be reliably reconstructed, handle it explicitly rather than inventing a score.

Where examination definitions change after an attempt, use the definition applicable to that attempt, not a newly edited version of the exam.

### 6.4 Letter grades and grade configuration

Inspect whether the application already has a grade-band configuration.

If it does, make sure the configured grading boundaries are respected consistently. If it doesn't, first fix the existing point allocation and total-mark integrity without introducing an unnecessary platform-wide grading configuration that could disrupt existing reports.

Any exam-specific grades or scales must be clearly defined and consistently applied.

