## 11. STANDARDISE CLASSES ACROSS THE ENTIRE PLATFORM

The current platform contains inconsistent class labels such as Year 5 appearing more than once or equivalent classes appearing under different naming systems.

We need one standard class system across all relevant portals and workflows.

The canonical class options should be exactly:

- Early Years
- Grade 1
- Grade 2
- Grade 3
- Grade 4
- Grade 5
- Grade 6
- Grade 7
- Grade 8
- Grade 9
- Grade 10
- Grade 11
- Grade 12

Use consistent spelling, casing and stored values. Do not create separate entries for different labels that represent the same class.

### 11.1 Centralise the class definitions

Inspect all places where class names or class options are defined.

Create or reuse a single shared class-definition utility or equivalent source of truth. Do not maintain independent lists with subtly different options in different components.

Apply the canonical list to every relevant area, including:

- Student enrolment and onboarding.
- School registration and class setup.
- Parent enrolment.
- Tutor student management.
- Examination creation and class assignment.
- Student examination eligibility.
- Schedules and timetables.
- Assignments and learning tracks.
- School reports and student progress.
- Attendance and other class-scoped workflows.
- Search filters and dropdowns.
- Any relevant exports, summaries and reporting interfaces.

Search for hardcoded class lists and labels across the repository. Fix the underlying reuse problem rather than changing only the dropdown currently visible.

### 11.2 Map legacy class names correctly

Existing records may contain names such as:

- Year 1, Year 2 and Year 5.
- Primary 1 through Primary 6.
- Basic 1 through Basic 9.
- JSS1, JSS2 and JSS3.
- SS1, SS2 and SS3.
- Grade 1 and other existing variants.
- Nursery or other Early Years labels.

Inspect the actual values used by this application and create an explicit, tested normalisation map.

Where the meaning is unambiguous, use the intended equivalence:

- Primary 1 / Basic 1 / Year 1 → Grade 1.
- Primary 5 / Basic 5 / Year 5 → Grade 5.
- JSS1 → Grade 7.
- JSS2 → Grade 8.
- JSS3 → Grade 9.
- SS1 → Grade 10.
- SS2 → Grade 11.
- SS3 → Grade 12.
- Existing nursery or early-childhood classes → Early Years, where appropriate.

Handle other genuine legacy labels according to their actual meaning. Do not blindly convert ambiguous values into the wrong grade.

Create one canonical representation for newly created or updated data while preserving compatibility with existing student records, schedules, examinations and enrolments.

### 11.3 Migrate historical records safely

Do not simply change the display label and leave backend eligibility checks comparing incompatible values.

Trace how class values are stored, queried and matched. Ensure a class assigned to an examination can still match the correct legacy student record after standardisation.

Plan and implement any required data normalisation or migration using the existing Firebase architecture.

The migration must be:

- Safe and repeatable.
- Restricted to the intended fields and collections.
- Compatible with existing valid records.
- Logged without exposing sensitive student information.
- Reversible or recoverable from an appropriate backup or recorded migration plan.
- Tested against representative legacy data before being applied broadly.

If the current environment does not have authorised access to production data, create and test the migration logic without pretending that the production migration has run.

Do not create duplicate canonical classes or merge different schools' class rosters merely because their class labels match. Class identity must remain appropriately scoped to the relevant school and academic context.

